@lang en
-- Source text: Interpretation Act 2005 (Ireland), Act No. 23 of 2005, as enacted, from the unofficial plain-text rendering No.-23-of-2005.txt.
-- (c) Government of Ireland. Oireachtas Copyright Material is reproduced with the permission of the Houses of the Oireachtas.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE IS.
-- The reusable time module for the Interpretation Act 2005 (Ireland): when an enactment comes into operation, how a period of days, weeks,
-- months or years is counted and where it ends, which day is its last, and what the Act's own time words mean.
-- It imports only `prelude` and `daydate`.
--
-- PRECONDITION, STATED ONCE (section 4(1)): "A provision of this Act applies to an enactment except in so far as the contrary intention
-- appears in this Act, in the enactment itself or, where relevant, in the Act under which the enactment is made."
-- Callers apply this module only where no contrary intention appears; no function here takes a contrary-intention flag.
--
-- WHERE THE TEXT IS SILENT THE MODULE REFUSES; it never answers with a plausible default.
-- The Act has no rule for: a period counted "after" an event; a period of months, years or weeks that begins on a day other than the first
-- day of a calendar month, of a year, or of a week; a last day that falls on a day of rest, recess or holiday; the moment within the date
-- of passing at which an Act with no commencement provision comes into operation.
-- Each of those is a named refusal below, and NOTES.md (fork register) says what each reading would answer.
--
-- TWO ASSUMPTIONS THE TEXT DOES NOT STATE.
-- (1) The Act does not define "day". A day is taken to be a calendar date of `daydate`, running from one midnight to the next.
-- (2) "midnight" is, in relation to a particular day, "the point of time at which the day ends". The end of one day and the start of the next
--     are taken to be the same point, so a provision in operation from the end of D-1 is in operation throughout D.
--
-- Days of rest, recess and holiday, and public holidays, are INPUTS (a function from a date to a boolean). No calendar of holidays is encoded.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

§ `Types`

-- src:86 | “Act” means—
-- src:87 | (a) an Act of the Oireachtas, and
-- src:88 | (b) a statute which was in force in Saorstát Éireann immediately before the date of the coming into operation of the Constitution and which continued in force by virtue of Article 50 of the Constitution;
-- src:91 | “statutory instrument” means an order, regulation, rule, bye-law, warrant, licence, certificate, direction, notice, guideline or other like document made, issued, granted or otherwise created by or under an Act and references, in relation to a statutory instrument, to “made” or to “made under” include references to made, issued, granted or otherwise created by or under such instrument.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 2(1) "Act", "statutory instrument" (src:86-88, src:91)
DECLARE `Kind of enactment` IS ONE OF
    `an Act of the Oireachtas`
    `a statute of Saorstat Eireann continued in force`
    `a statutory instrument`

-- src:230 | “midnight” means, in relation to a particular day, the point of time at which the day ends;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 Schedule Pt 1 "midnight" (src:230)
DECLARE Midnight HAS
    `the day it ends` IS A DATE

-- src:155 | Commencement. 16.—(1) Subject to subsection (2), every provision of an Act comes into operation on the date of its passing.
-- src:156 | (2) Where an Act or a provision of an Act is expressed to come into operation on a particular day (whether the day is before or after the date of the passing of the Act and whether the day is named in the Act or is to be fixed or ascertained in a particular manner), the Act or provision comes into operation at the end of the day before the particular day.
-- src:157 | (3) Subject to subsection (4), every provision of a statutory instrument comes into operation at the end of the day before the day on which the statutory instrument is made.
-- src:158 | (4) Where a statutory instrument or a provision of a statutory instrument is expressed to come into operation on a particular day (whether the day is before or after the date of the making of the statutory instrument and whether the day is named in the instrument or is to be fixed or ascertained in a particular manner), the statutory instrument or provision comes into operation at the end of the day before the particular day.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 ss 16(1)-(4) (src:155-158)
DECLARE `What an enactment says about its own commencement` IS ONE OF
    `it says nothing about when it comes into operation`
    `it is expressed to come into operation on a particular day`  HAS `the particular day` IS A DATE
    `it is expressed to come into operation on a day not yet fixed or ascertained`

-- src:153 | Date of passing of Acts of Oireachtas. 15.—(1) The date of the passing of an Act of the Oireachtas is the date of the day on which the Bill for the Act is signed by the President.
-- src:155 | Commencement. 16.—(1) Subject to subsection (2), every provision of an Act comes into operation on the date of its passing.
-- src:156 | (2) Where an Act or a provision of an Act is expressed to come into operation on a particular day (whether the day is before or after the date of the passing of the Act and whether the day is named in the Act or is to be fixed or ascertained in a particular manner), the Act or provision comes into operation at the end of the day before the particular day.
-- src:157 | (3) Subject to subsection (4), every provision of a statutory instrument comes into operation at the end of the day before the day on which the statutory instrument is made.
-- src:158 | (4) Where a statutory instrument or a provision of a statutory instrument is expressed to come into operation on a particular day (whether the day is before or after the date of the making of the statutory instrument and whether the day is named in the instrument or is to be fixed or ascertained in a particular manner), the statutory instrument or provision comes into operation at the end of the day before the particular day.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 ss 15(1), 16 (src:153, src:155-158)
DECLARE `Facts about an enactment` HAS
    `its kind`                            IS A `Kind of enactment`
    `what it says about commencement`     IS A `What an enactment says about its own commencement`
    `the day of its passing or making`    IS A MAYBE DATE

-- src:155 | Commencement. 16.—(1) Subject to subsection (2), every provision of an Act comes into operation on the date of its passing.
-- src:156 | (2) Where an Act or a provision of an Act is expressed to come into operation on a particular day (whether the day is before or after the date of the passing of the Act and whether the day is named in the Act or is to be fixed or ascertained in a particular manner), the Act or provision comes into operation at the end of the day before the particular day.
-- src:157 | (3) Subject to subsection (4), every provision of a statutory instrument comes into operation at the end of the day before the day on which the statutory instrument is made.
-- src:158 | (4) Where a statutory instrument or a provision of a statutory instrument is expressed to come into operation on a particular day (whether the day is before or after the date of the making of the statutory instrument and whether the day is named in the instrument or is to be fixed or ascertained in a particular manner), the statutory instrument or provision comes into operation at the end of the day before the particular day.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 ss 16(1)-(4) (src:155-158)
DECLARE Commencement HAS
    `the day the text names`      IS A DATE
    `the moment the text fixes`   IS A MAYBE Midnight
    `the provision that decides`  IS A STRING

DECLARE `Unit of time` IS ONE OF
    Days
    Weeks
    `Calendar months`
    Years

DECLARE `How a period is counted` IS ONE OF
    `from a day`
    `after an event`

DECLARE `A period of time` HAS
    `its length`                   IS A NUMBER
    `its unit`                     IS A `Unit of time`
    `how it is counted`            IS A `How a period is counted`
    `the day it is counted from`   IS A DATE

DECLARE Span HAS
    `its first day` IS A DATE
    `its last day`  IS A DATE

§ `Named refusals`

-- One definition per refusal; each name is its message. A refusal is an expression at any type, so no sentinel value is needed.

-- src:177 | (h) Periods of time. Where a period of time is expressed to begin on or be reckoned from a particular day, that day shall be deemed to be included in the period and, where a period of time is expressed to end on or be reckoned to a particular day, that day shall be deemed to be included in the period;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 18(h) (src:177)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 18(h) says nothing about a period counted after an event` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 18(h) says nothing about a period counted after an event"

